Answer: 9 combinations

Let's find the coordinates of the intersection point of the two graphs. Since we know the graphs intersect at $(2,2)$, $(4,6)$, $(6,12)$, and $(8,12)$, we know that when $x=2, y=2$. Now the graphs are at the same $\large{2\large{y}}$ version, so we only use the point with the higher $y$ value to solve the new problem.
$$
y=2^{3x} \to 2^{3x} = 2y \\
y=j(x)\to 2^{3x}=2\times j(x) \\
2^{3x}=2\times j(x)\\
j(x)=2^{3x-1}
$$
Now, we have the following table:
\begin{tabular}{cc}
$2(x)$ & $1(2x)$ \\
\hline
$2$ & $2^{3(2)-1} = 32$
\end{tabular}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
$2(x)$ & $2(2x)$ \\
\hline
$4$ & $2^{3(4)-1} = 512$
\end{tabular}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
$2(x)$ & $3(2x)$ \\
\hline
$6$ & $2^{3(6)-1} = 8192$
\end{tabular}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
$2(x)$ & $4(2x)$ \\
\hline
$8$ & $2^{3(8)-1} = 16384$
\end{tabular}
\begin{tabular}{cc}
$x$ & $2^{3(2x)-1}$ \\
\hline
$8$ & $2^{23}$
\end{tabular}
So, the coordinates for the intersection are $(8,\large{2^{23}})$, and the sum of these two is $\boxed{2^{23}+8}$. Q.E.D.
